## 1. Introduction
Graphdiyne (GDY), with high degrees of $\pi$ -conjugated carbon ma­ terial, is a flat material with $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ -and sp hybridized carbon atoms, which has attracted great consideration from fundamental studies and prac­ tical applications because of new physical and chemical properties (Li et al., 2019; Yu et al., 2019). Two-dimensional layers of GDY composed of benzene rings and acetylene units, due to the more suitable band gap for semiconductors relative to graphene, high electrical conductivity, better dispersion in aqueous solution, uniformly distributed pore structure and high chemical, electrochemical and thermal stability (Wu et al., 2020), has been expressed extensively performance in promising applications, such as catalysis (Zhang et al., 2015), lithium-ion battery storage (Huang et al., 2015), electrocatalytic oxygen reduction (Zhao et al., 2018), sensors (Zhuang et al., 2017), antimicrobial (Gu et al.,
2017), biological protection and cancer therapy (Jin et al., 2018). Furthermore, theoretical studies using the Density-functional theory (DFT) confirms the suitability of GDY for catalytic process, electrode materials preparation and semiconductor devices fabrication (Wang et al., 2018). In addition, GDY can be used as efficient platform for development of sensitive sensors and biosensors. Between different sensing systems such as electrochemical immunoassay (Yang et al., 2015), enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) (Parker et al., 1992), radioimmunoassay (Song et al., 2015), fluorescence assay (Alarfaj et al., 2018) and electrogenerated chemiluminescence (ECL), the ECL has the privileged merit of both electrochemical and chemilu­ minescent assay such as high sensitivity and specificity, low background signal, simple instrumentation, extended dynamic range, and low cost (Babamiri et al., 2018a, 2018b, 2019).
As one of the most extensively studied ECL luminophore, $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)_{3}}}^{2+}$ possesses distinct advantages such as high quantum yield and ECL effi­ ciency, good photochemical, and chemical stability (Babamiri et al., 2017). The $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)_{3}}}^{2+}$ /tripropylamine (TPrA) system is one of the most common ECL systems in commercial ECL immunoassays because of high ECL efficiency, good biocompatibility and good water solubility (Zhang et al., 2019). However, TPrA itself has a lot of disadvantages such as toxicity, corrosive, flammability, which led to efforts to exploit alter­ native co-reactants (Li et al., 2015). Nitrogen-doped carbon nanodots because of matching redox potentials with $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)_{3}}}^{2+}$ , high chemical stability, good solubility, swift charge transfer kinetics and rapid degradation routes, can be utilized as an appropriate alternative to the TPrA (Carrara et al., 2017). Also, NCND can covalently bonded to metal-organic complex to achieve the self-enhanced ECL for increasing the anodic ECL response owing to the shorter electron transfer route and less energy loss (Bahari et al., 2020). On the other hand, the $\mathsf{s m}S_{2}$ QDs can facilitated the generation of $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)_{3}}}^{2+}$ radicals and generate the excited state of $\operatorname{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}^{2+\ast}$ , then leading to generate strong emission and it can be used as new and promising co-reactant of $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)}}_{3}\mathrm{{}}^{2+}$ (Xing et al., 2018). Furthermore, one of the most general strategies for the amplification of the ECL response is the increasing of loading capacity and the immobilizing abundant of luminophores. Metal-organic frame­ works (MOFs) are porous hybrid materials with a periodic network structure composed of modular self-assembly of nodes (metal ions or clusters) and organic bridging ligands. MOFs with large surface areas and well-defined uniform nano-scale pores and channels (Cai et al., 2018), satisfactory electrochemical stabilities (Zhou et al., 2019), adjustable composition, high permanent and easy to assemble have been widely applied in energy storage, catalysis, analytical detection, and biosensing (Lv et al., 2020a). Among these applications, the develop­ ment of sensing systems based on MOF has attracted great attention (Ren et al., 2018; Lv et al., 2018, 2020b).
The sensitive ECL sensors and biosensors are developed by encap­ sulation or loading of $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)_{3}}}^{2+}$ or its derivatives onto MOFs (Huang et al., 2018). However, MOFs probably have non-conductive shells that hinder electron excitation of the luminophores (Carrara et al., 2017), and decreased the ECL efficiency. To overcome this constraint, various nanomaterials such as graphene oxide (GO) (Peng et al., 2018), and carbon nanotubes (CNTs) (Jabbari et al., 2016), have been used as the sensing platform to improve the electric conductivity of MOFs and ECL efficiency.
Carbohydrate antigen 19-9 (CA19-9) as a kind of glycoprotein associated with malignant tumors is an isolated Lewis cancer biomarker of MUC1 protein for pancreatic cancer, urothelial carcinomas, colorectal cancer and gastric carcinoma, especially pancreatic carcinoma (Zhang et al., 2016), so, development of sensitive and selective method for CA19-9 detection in clinical diagnostics is a challenge.
In this study, for the first time, graphdiyne (GDY) as the novel sensing platform integrated with MOF enriched Ru-complexes as a signal probe for development of sensitive self-enhanced ECL immuno­ sensor toward CA19-9 tumor marker detection as summarized the schematic 1. Furthermore, the sensitivity of the measuring method improved by both NCNDs, which covalently attached onto Ru-complex and also $\mathsf{s m}\mathsf{S}_{2}$ QDs loaded onto MOF as co-reactants. The performane of the proposed platform was demonstrated by both theoretical and experimental methods. Herein, MOF encapsulated by the Ru complex and decorated with Ru-NCNDs complex was applied as the carrier for loading more luminophores and $\mathsf{s m}S_{2}$ QD decorated onto MOF applied as coreactant for improving of ECL response. The $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MOF@NCNDs{\cdot}R u}$ $\textcircled{a}\ S\mathrm{m}S_{2}$ QD located on the surface of Nafion/GDY modified ITO elec­ trode and after the immobilization of the primary antibody $(\mathsf{A b}_{1})$ and incubation of CA19-9 antigens, the ECL signal measured. The applica­ tion of the presented assay for CA19-9 tumor marker detection in real samples was also evaluated. The proposed immunoassay could be developed for establishing other immunoassays and measuring of different cancer markers with relevant antigens and antibodies.
