## 3. Results and discussions
## 3.1. Characterization of NCND, [Ru-NCND] and [MOF-Ru-NCNDs]
## 3.2. Characterization of $s m S_{2}$ QD
The size distribution and the morphological features of the $\mathsf{s m}S_{2}$ QDs were characterized by taking of TEM image (Fig. 1F). As can be seen, the as-prepared $\mathsf{s m}S_{2}$ QDs are uniform spherical particles with an average size of $6{-}8\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Also, the characterization of $\mathsf{s m}\mathsf{S}_{2}$ QDs with other spectroscopic thecniques including, UV–vis, PL and FT-IR are reported in supporting information file (SI).
3.3. Characterization of GDY and its effects on the ECL response of Ru (bpy)2(phen-NH2) 2+
The SEM and TEM techniques were utilized for characterization morphology and structure of as-synthesized GDY. The SEM image in Fig. 1G indicated that GDY was composed of uniform and continuous microstructure with accumulated layers and the TEM image of the prepared GDY have the lamellar structure (Fig. 1H). Furthermore, XPS measurements were done to characterize the functional groups of GDY and to further confirm the existence of the elements. In the full XPS spectrum of the as-prepared GDY two characteristic peaks at 281.3 and 528.1 eV was observed, which were corresponded to the C1s and O1s binding peak, respectively (Fig. 1I). The expanded XPS spectra of C1s composed of four sub-peaks at 284.4, 285.1, 286.4 and $287.5\mathrm{eV}$ that can be assigned to carbon in the form of $\mathsf{c}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ $(\mathsf{s p}^{2}\cdot$ ), $\mathtt{C}\equiv\mathtt{C}$ (sp), C–OH, and $\mathsf{C}{=}0$ , respectively (Fig. 1J). The observed C–O and $\mathtt{C=}0$ peaks in GDY may be due to the adsorption of oxygen on the surface of GDY and the chemical reaction of oxygen with the terminal acetylene bonds.
