## 3.5. Possible ECL mechanism
Previous studies have shown that the NCNDs can be utilized as a new anodic coreactant for the ECL emission of Ru(II)-complex (Carrara et al., 2017). Furthermore, for improving the ECL intensity signal, the $\mathsf{s m}\mathsf{S}_{2}$ QD as a novel coreactant toward $\mathrm{{Ru(II)}}$ -complex based ECL system was introduced. In addition, the ECL intensity of $\mathrm{{Ru(II)}}$ -complex in the presence of dual co-reactants (NCND and $\mathsf{s m}\mathsf{S}_{2}$ QD) was higher than those of in NCND or $\mathsf{s m}\mathsf{S}_{2}$ QD separately. The $\mathrm{NCND^{\bullet^{+}}}$ and $\mathrm{Sm}\mathrm{S}_{2}\mathrm{OD}^{\bullet+}$ cation radicals were generated by charge injection during potential cycling from 0.0 to $1.5\mathrm{V}$ ,at the same time, $\mathrm{,Ru(bpy)_{2}(p h e n–N H_{2})}$ $^{2+}$ was oxidized to $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)_{2}(p h e n{-}N H_{2})_{2}}^{3+}}$ . According to previous reported studies (Huang et al., 2015; Yang et al., 2019) the probable ECL mech­ anisms are shown as follows:
$$
\mathrm{NCND-Ru(bpy)_{2}(p h e n-N H_{2})^{2+}-e^{-}\rightarrow N C N D-R u(b p y)_{2}(p h e n-N H_{2})^{3+}~(e l e c t a r)_{3}(e l e c t a r)_{4}(e l e c t a r)_{5}}
$$
$$
\mathrm{\DeltaDH\cdotRu(bpy)_{2}(p h e n{-}N H_{2})^{2+}{\mathrm{-}}e^{-}\to N C N D H^{\bullet+}{\mathrm{-}}R u(b p y)_{2}(p h e n{-}}
$$
$$
\mathrm{NCNDH^{\bullet\bullet}\mathrm{-Ru(bpy)_{2}(p h e n\mathrm{-}N H_{2})^{2+}\mathrm{-}\nabla H^{+}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{\rightarrow\mathrm{NCND^{\bullet}\mathrm{-}R u(b p y)_{2}(p h e n\mathrm{-}N H_{2})^{2+}\mathrm{-}\nabla h^{+}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{\rightarrow\mathrm{}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{\Omega_{2}(p h e n\mathrm{-}N H_{2})^{2+}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{\Omega_{2}(p h e n\mathrm{-}N H_{2})^{2}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{}\Omega_{2}(p h e n\mathrm{-}N H_{2})^{2}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{}\Omega_{2}(p h e n\mathrm{-}N H_{2})^{2}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{}\Omega_{2}(p h e n\mathrm{-}N H_{2})^{2}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{}\Omega_{2}(p h e n\mathrm{-}N H_{2})^{2}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{}\Omega_{2}(p h e n\mathrm{-}N H_{2})^{2}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{}\Omega_{2}(p h e n\mathrm{-}N H_{2})^{2}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{}\Omega_{2}^{+}\mathrm{}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{}\Omega_{2}^{+}\mathrm{}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{}\Omega_{2}^{+}\mathrm{}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{}\mathrm{~NCND}^{\bullet\mathrm{}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{}R u(b p y)_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{}\Omega_{2}(p h e n\mathrm{-}N H_{2})^{2}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{}\Omega_{2}^{+}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{}\Omega_{2}^{+}\mathrm{-}
$$
$\mathrm{Sm}{\mathrm{S}_{2}}$ Q $\mathrm{D-e^{-}}\to\mathrm{SmS}_{2}\mathrm{QD}^{\bullet+}$ (electrode oxidation)
$$
\mathrm{R^{\cdot}+N C N D-R u(b p y)_{2}(p h e n-N H_{2})^{3+}\rightarrow N C N D-R u(b p y)_{2}(p h e n-N H_{2})^{2+}*+R}
$$
$$
\mathrm{\partial\cdotRu(bpy)_{2}(p h e n\mathrm{-NH_{2}})^{2+}\mathrm{\rightarrow\mathrm{NCND\mathrm{-Ru(bpy)_{2}(p h e n\mathrm{-}N H_{2})^{2+}+\mathrm{h}\nu}}}}}
$$
(R represented dual coreactant NCND and $s\mathrm{m}S_{2}$ QD).
## 3.6. Comparison of the ECL responses with different signal probes
Recently, in situ generating coreactant or synthesizing self-enhanced $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)_{3}}}^{2+}$ derivative with NCNDs as coreactant was developed in ECL systems. Fig. 3B demonstrates the ECL intensity in the presence of NCNDs as co-reactant when covalently linked to $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)_{2}(p h e n–N H_{2})}$ in comparison to uncoupled components. As illustrated for NCNDs-Ru (curve b), the ECL signal response $120\%$ increase compared to uncou­ pled NCNDs and Ru-complex (curve a). So, the intermolecular electron transfers between $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)_{2}(p h e n{\cdot}N H_{2})^{+2}}}$ and the NCNDs in NCNDs- Rucomplex decreased the electron transfer distance compared to the intramolecular condition when Ru-complex and NCNDs components are individual. Furthermore, MOFs can be served as new nanomaterial to immobilize abundant $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)_{3}}}^{2+}$ derivatives for improvement of ECL systems efficiency (Wang et al., 2019; Shao et al., 2018). As illustrated in Fig. 3C, the ECL emission intensity of $\mathrm{Ru}@$ MOF@NCNDs (curve b) and MOF@NCNDs-Ru (curve c) are 2.07 times and 2.54 times higher respect to Ru-NCNDs (curve a). When MOFs encapsulated with the Ru $\mathrm{(bpy)_{2}(p h e n{\cdot}N H_{2})^{+2}}$ and decorated with Ru-NCND, the difference be­ tween ECL intensity for Ru@MOF@NCND-Ru and Ru-NCND was about 370 a.u. (Fig. 3D), which increased 1.71 folds compared to MOF $@$ NCND-Ru platform. This result indicated the covalently linking of NCND with $\mathrm{Ru(bpy)_{2}(p h e n–N H_{2})}$ ) complex and also decoration of Ru-NCND complexes on ${\mathrm{Ru}}@{\mathrm{MOF}}$ effectively improve the analytical performance of the proposed ECL immunosensor due to the shorter electron transfer distance and loading large amount of luminophore. Finally, when the $\mathsf{s m}\mathsf{s}_{2}$ QD loaded onto $\mathsf{R u}@\mathsf{M O F@N C N C-R u}.$ the ECL signal of the immunosensor due to facilitate the generating of Ru $(\mathrm{bpy})_{2}(\mathrm{phen}{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ 2+ radicals was enhanced (curve c). So, synergistic effects of NCNDs covalently attached onto Ru-complex and also $\mathsf{s m}\mathsf{S}_{2}$ QDs loaded onto MOF improved the ECL response of the probe.
## 3.7. The ECL efficiency of Ru(bpy)2(phen-NH2)@MOF@NCND- Ru (bpy)2(phen-NH2) platform
ECL quantum efficiency is distinguished as the ratio of the number of emitted photons to electrons transferred during the chemiluminescent procedure. The ECL efficiency of $\operatorname{Ru}@\operatorname{MOF}@\operatorname{NCND}.$ Ru was calculated using the $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)_{3}}]^{2+}/\mathrm{TPrA}$ system as the standard through the following relation(Cui et al., 2020), at scan rate of $100\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ :
$$
Q=\left.\frac{\mathcal{D}_{x}}{\mathcal{D}_{0}}=[\frac{\int_{0}^{t}I d t}{\int_{0}^{t}i d t}]_{x}\right/[\frac{\int_{0}^{t}I d t}{\int_{0}^{t}i d t}]_{0}
$$
Here, $\varnothing_{\mathbf{x}}$ represents the ECL efficiency of Ru@MOF@NCND-Ru, $\scriptstyle\varnothing_{0}$ represents the ECL efficiency of $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)_{3}}]^{2+}/\mathrm{TPrA}_{\mathrm{\Omega}}$ , I stand for ECL intensity, and i refer to the current value. The relative ECL efficiency is calculated to be $13.34\%$ which comparable or better than some ECL platforms (Table S2). Also, the ECL efficiency of ${\mathrm{Ru}}@{\mathrm{MOF}}$ , MOF@NCNDs-Ru and NCNDs-NCNDs-Ru were 8.46, 10.17 and $2.65\%$ respectively. Obviously, the ECL efficiency of $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MOF@NCND-Ru}$ is relatively high, due to increase the loading amount of luminophores in MOF network and also increasing of electron transfer kinetic for inter­ amolecular reaction of NCND-Ru.
## 3.8. Optimization of experimental conditions
In order to obtain the high performances of the fabricated ECL immunosensor, the experimental parameters including the incubation time of CA 19-9 antigen with related antibody, the concentration of NCNDs and $\mathsf{s m}\mathsf{S}_{2}$ QD were optimized and details presented in SI.
## 3.9. Characterization of the immunosensor
To characterize the stepwise modification process of the immuno­ sensor, the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) is an effective approach. The ITO electrode modified with nafion/graphdiyne has a small semicircle, indicating that graphdiyne had good conductivity (Fig. 4A, curve a). After that $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MOF@NCND-Ru}@S\mathrm{m}S_{2}$ QD decorated on the surface of the modified electrode, the electron-transfer resistance $\mathrm{(R_{et})}$ increased because the formed $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MOF@NCND-Ru}@S\mathrm{m}S_{2}$ QD largely prevented the electron transport (curve b). When $\mathsf{A b}_{1}$ bonded on the MOF enriched ruthenium (II), the electron transfer resistance increased, owing to the fact that $\mathbf{Ab}_{1}$ obstructed the electron transfer (curve c). Then, increases of the $\mathrm{R_{et}}$ were attained by incubation of BSA, which can be ascribed to blocking property of BSA (curve d). Finally, incubating of the modified electrode with CA19-9 antigen, leading to increase the value of $\mathrm{\sfR}_{\mathrm{et}}$ because the CA19-9 antigen hindered the electron transfer of redox probe (curve e). In order to further prove that the proposed immunosensor was fabricated successfully, the stepwise fabrication process of the immunosensor was also characterized by ECL (Fig. S8). Compared with the bare electrode (curve a), the ECL response was enhanced greatly after $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MOF@NCNDs–Ru}@S\mathrm{m}S_{2}$ QDs/graph­ diyne was immobilized on the surface of ITO electrode (curve b). Sub­ sequently, a gradual decrease in the ECL signal was achieved when CA 19-9 antibody (curve c), BSA (curve d) and CA 19-9 antigens (curve e) was modified onto the electrode surface successively, indicating the electron transfer was hindered by the isolated biomacromolecules. Both impedance and ECL results clearly demonstrated the prosperous fabri­ cation of the proposed ECL immunosensor.
## 3.11. Selectivity, stability and reproducibility of the ECL immunosensor
As indicated in Fig. 4B, negligible change of ECL signal for some tumor markers such as CA15-3, CA125, AFP, CEA and also HBsAg was obtained, while the ECL change response for CA19-9 antigens is flagrant relative to that of other antigens, that revealing the superior selectivity of the ECL immunosensor for CA19-9 measurement. Additionally, the interference of co-existing compounds in real samples such as glucose, uric acid and cholesterol on the immunosensor response was also investigated. As indicated in Fig. S9 interference effect of the proposed biololcules in CA 19-9 antigen detection in negligible, so, the introduced immunoassay is highly selective towards the detection of CA 19-9 in serum samples. The reproducibility of the constructed immunosensor was checked by measuring the ECL intensity of CA19-9 antigen (0.15 ${\mathrm{UmL}}^{-1}$ using five parallel tests. The calculated RSD value was $4.31\%$ that indicated an acceptable reproducibility of the ECL immunosensor. To appraise the precision of the ECL assay, the ECL intensities of the proposed immunosensor was measured during 10 consequence potential scan. As shown in Fig. 4C, the ECL intensities exhibit no distinct change and the calculated relative standard deviation (RSD) was $2.02\%$ , that showing the outstanding stability of the present immunosensor towards CA19-9 antigen analysis. In addition, when the immunosensor stored in $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 3 weeks, the current response declines to approximately $97\%$ of its initial response. This result indicated the good stability of the fabri­ cated immunosensor.
## 3.12. Analytical performance of the ECL immunosensor in CA19-9 detection
Under the optimal experimental conditions, the proposed imunosensor was used for quantitative measuring of antigen at different concentration. As demonstrated in Fig. 4D, the ECL intensities gradually decreased with increasing concentration of CA19-9 from $0.0005\mathrm{U}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ to $200~\mathrm{U~mL^{-1}}$ . The calibration curve exhibited a good linear relation­ ship between ECL signals and the logarithm of the CA19-9 antigen concentrations (inset Fig. 4D). The corresponding linear regression equation of ECL signals was $\Delta\mathrm{I}=110.5\mathrm{lg}\mathrm{[c/(U/mL)]}$ $+499.01(\mathrm{R}^{2}= $ 0.9908) (where $\Delta{\mathrm I}$ is the difference between the ECL intensity in the absence and presence of analyte and c is the concentration of CA19-9) and the calculated detection limit was $0.00013\mathrm{~U~mL}^{-1}$ $(S/\mathbb{N}=3)$ . The obtained analytical characteristics of the presented immnosensor are comparable or better than presented values for other CA19-9 bio­ sensors (Table S3). It was found that the fabricated ECL immunosensor has wider linear detection range and relatively lower detection limit, which demonstrated this proposed assay, has the excellent sensitivity for accurate measurement of CA19-9 tumor marker.
## 3.13. Analytical application of the immunosensor in real samples
To validate the applicability and feasibility of this ECL immunoassay, the recovery testing in the human serum containing CA19-9 antigen was examined and the obtained results were compared with ELISA as the standard method (Table 1). The results obviously indicated a good agreement between obtained results from introduced method and ELISA as a standard method.Furthermore, the accuracy of the obtained results was evaluated by human serum samples spiked with standard values of CA19-9 antigen. In this work, human serum samples were obtained from the Nour Clinical laboratory in Sanandaj of Iran. Since the linear range of the immunosensor was from $0.0005\mathrm{U}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ to $200\mathrm{U}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , so the two serum samples were diluted with phosphate buffer solution $\mathrm{(pH7.4)}$ . As shown in Table 1, $0.5\mathrm{U}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , $10\mathrm{U}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ and $50\mathrm{U}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ of CA19-9 were added to the human serum specimens. The recoveries were in the range of $97.85\%-101.55\%$ and $98.12\%-101.3\%$ with the RSD of $2.45\%-$ $3.62\%$ . The satisfactory results illustrated that our proposed ECL immunosensor has acceptable accuracy for the determination of CA19-9 tumor marker in serum samples in clinical diagnostics.
## 4. Conclusion
In this study, an integrated system containing MOF enriched Rucomplexes, and Ru-complexes covalently attached to NCNDs and $\mathsf{s m}\mathsf{S}_{2}$ QD as luminophores and coreactants, and GDY as electrochemical platform which impressively accelerated the electron transfer was used as novel label-free self-enhanced ECL system. The proposed triple signal amplification strategy was introduced for the fabrication of immuno­ sensor and its application in tumor marker detection. The NCND, which covalently linked to Ru-complex as effective coreactant, has been improved the ECL efficiency due to increasing the intramolecular elec­ tron transfer and decreasing of emitter-coreactant electron transfer path. Furthermore, decoration of $\mathsf{s m}\mathsf{S}_{2}$ QD as a supporting coreactor onto $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{MOF}@$ NCNDs-Ru was applied for improving of ECL efficiency. The ECL signal-amplified strategy was improved in the presence of graph­ diyne (GDY) nanosheets as a novel and efficient platform for acceler­ ating the electron transfer due to decreasing the band gap of Rucomplex, and this result has been proven by DFT calculations. The proposed ECL platform was used for immobilization of anti-CA19-9 antibody and fabrication of stable, sensitive and selective immuno­ sensor for CA19-9 tumor marker analysis at the broad linear range with low detection limit. In view of these advantages, the integrated ECL platform as an attractive candidate provided a new perspective for the development of high-performance ECL based assays and their applica­ tions in fabrication of laboratory biodevices, biological analysis and clinical diagnosis.
